    "excerpt": "Most students sit down to study for hours and end up scrolling their phones. The problem is not laziness. It is that the brain is not designed for long, uninter…",
    "body": [
      "Most students sit down to study for hours and end up scrolling their phones. The problem is not laziness. It is that the brain is not designed for long, uninterrupted focus. Attention works in pulses. The most effective study method respects that rhythm.",
      "It is called the Pomodoro Technique. Choose one topic. Set a timer for 25 minutes. Remove every distraction. Study only that topic until the timer rings. Then take a 5-minute break. Stand up. Stretch. Drink water. Do not check social media during the break. After four cycles, take a longer 20-minute break.",
      "Why 25 minutes? Research shows that sustained attention drops sharply after about 20 to 30 minutes. Short bursts followed by breaks keep your brain fresh. Each cycle is a full unit of concentrated effort. You will cover more in two hours of focused Pomodoro sessions than in four hours of distracted reading.",
      "This method works for any subject. Use it for JAMB revision. Use it for WAEC past questions. Use it when learning a new skill. The key is discipline during the 25-minute block. If a stray thought enters your mind, write it on a notepad and return to it later. Train your focus like a muscle.",
      "Start today. Pick one topic you have been avoiding. Set your timer. You will be surprised how much you can learn in 25 minutes of true focus."
    ],
